EXT. GLACIER CREVASSE - DAY

Blue walls. Forty feet of ice above, a slot of white sky. Wind whistles across the top like a flute.

At the bottom, on a ledge no wider than a door: KWABENA ADDO, 40s, Ghanaian geologist, burly, beard crusted with frost, left leg bent wrong at the shin.

A rope dangles ten feet above. Swinging. Cut at the end.

Two meters below him, on a lower shelf: BJORN HALVORSEN, 50s, Norwegian guide, lean as a rake, gray eyes, bleeding from his scalp. He doesn't move.

KWABENA
Bjorn.

Nothing.

KWABENA (CONT'D)
Bjorn!

The wind. A dripping sound.

Bjorn's hand twitches. Opens. Closes.

BJORN
(hoarse)
Don't shout. The ice listens.

KWABENA
You're alive.

BJORN
Don't sound so disappointed.

He rolls to his side. Pushes up on one elbow. Blood runs down his cheek. He wipes it with a glove, stares at the red.

KWABENA
Can you climb?

BJORN
Can you?

KWABENA
My leg.

BJORN
Then no.

Bjorn crawls to the lip of his shelf. Looks up the wall. His breath fogs. He reaches out and grips a protrusion of ice, tests it with his weight.

It holds.

KWABENA
He cut the rope.

BJORN
Who?

KWABENA
Anders. He let go on purpose.

BJORN
He was scared.

KWABENA
He was a coward.

BJORN
Same thing, down here.

Silence. Drips. Far off, a deep CRACK rolls through the glacier like a door slamming in a house the size of a mountain.

Both men freeze.

KWABENA
That's close.

BJORN
Don't breathe.

They don't. Ice dust sifts down. Settles on Kwabena's eyelashes. On Bjorn's shoulders. The crack fades.

BJORN (CONT'D)
Okay. Now you breathe.

Kwabena lets out a ragged exhale, laughs, coughs.

KWABENA
You Norwegians.

BJORN
You Ghanaians.

KWABENA
What about us?

BJORN
You laugh when you should be afraid.

KWABENA
I laugh because I am afraid.

Bjorn considers this. Nods. Unslings his pack. Digs.

He pulls out: a coil of thin cord, two ice screws, a pocket knife, half a chocolate bar.

BJORN
Inventory.

KWABENA
That's all?

BJORN
That's all.

He tosses the chocolate. Kwabena catches it one-handed. Breaks it in two. Throws half back.

KWABENA
Your turn to eat.

BJORN
You're hurt worse.

KWABENA
Eat.

Bjorn eats. Chews slowly. Studies the wall.

BJORN
There's a lip. Twelve feet up. Past it, a chimney.

KWABENA
You can see that?

BJORN
I can see the shadow of it.

He clips an ice screw to his belt. Twists the second between his fingers like a pen.

BJORN (CONT'D)
I'll go up. Anchor. Drop the cord.

KWABENA
And if the ice breaks?

BJORN
Then you'll have the chocolate.

Kwabena shakes his head, grinning in spite of himself.

Bjorn plants a boot. Presses a glove to the wall. Begins to climb, metal clicking on blue.

Six feet. Eight. He stops. He's breathing hard, forehead against the ice.

KWABENA
Bjorn.

BJORN
Quiet.

Another CRACK, closer. Bjorn flattens against the wall. Kwabena braces his good leg against the ledge.

The wall shudders. A sheet of ice the size of a car door sheers away from the opposite side and shatters on the shelf between them.

Silence. Both men stare at the wreckage.

BJORN (CONT'D)
(quiet)
Now.

He climbs. Fast now. Ten feet. Eleven. His hand finds the lip, grips, pulls. He rolls over the top of the ledge and disappears.

Kwabena waits. Seconds. A minute.

Then the cord drops, snaking down, dancing in the draft. It lands across his lap.

BJORN (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Tie in. Slowly.

Kwabena ties the cord around his chest, hands trembling. He tugs it twice. Tugs again.

KWABENA
Ready.

BJORN (O.S.)
Say it in your language.

KWABENA
Say what?

BJORN (O.S.)
The thing you say before you do something stupid.

KWABENA
(smiling)
Nyame nti.

BJORN (O.S.)
That. Do that.

The cord goes tight. Kwabena lifts off the ledge, swinging, grunting, and rises into the white.
